{
  "configuration_id": "config_027",
  "reason": "We have a single baseline (config_005: rna_only, singleprofile, scgpt → acc 0.411, F1 0.378), leaving major uncertainty about whether the image modality contributes any predictive signal. config_027 (multimodal, singleprofile, scgpt, imagenet_vit_l, no aggregation, concat fusion) is the minimal structural change from config_005: it keeps the same RNA encoder (scgpt), the same profile setting (singleprofile), and adds only the image branch with a simple concat fusion (which is structurally necessary once a second modality is present). This directly isolates the effect of adding image information. If config_027 substantially exceeds 0.411, image adds value and we can then explore which image encoder, aggregation, or fusion strategy matters most. If it performs similarly or worse, we learn that the image modality (at least with this encoder/fusion) is not helping, redirecting budget toward RNA-side improvements (e.g., multiprofile, alternative encoders). Under a tight budget, resolving whether the second modality is worth pursuing is the highest-value uncertainty to reduce before committing experiments to finer-grained design choices."
}

